From e45dd46f09e42a9da3863f8406c4f587465151c9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jelle Jassies <jellejassies@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 3 Apr 2026 18:21:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] fix: split heartbeat into two UDP datagrams to fix DC
 latency display

Dante Controller (DC) was not showing latency statistics (peak/average/late)
for Inferno devices. Investigation by packet capture analysis comparing
Inferno against a Shure MXWANI8 reference device revealed the root cause.

## Root cause

Inferno was sending all heartbeat blocks in a single combined UDP packet.
Dante Controller requires two *separate* UDP datagrams per second, each
containing a specific subset of blocks:

  Datagram 1 (util):    [0x8000 utilization] + [0x8001 clock PPB offset]
  Datagram 2 (latency): [0x8003 rx latency]  + [0x8004 missed packets]

When all blocks arrive in one datagram, DC ignores the latency data entirely
and displays nothing in the peak/average/late columns.

## Changes

- send_heartbeat() now performs two UDP sends per tick (one per group)
- 0x8002 (audio peaks) removed from heartbeats: the Shure reference device
  never includes this block in multicast heartbeats
- Each block group now uses an independent seqnum counter (util_block_seqnum
  and latency_block_seqnum), matching the Shure pattern
- heartbeat_tick field removed (alternating logic no longer needed)
- Fixed: RwLockReadGuard was held across an .await point; now dropped
  inside an explicit block scope before the send, satisfying the Send bound
  required by tokio::spawn

## Verification

Tested on physical hardware:
- Inferno RX node (Arch Linux, software PTP via Statime)
- Dante grandmaster: Shure MXWANI8
- Dante Controller on Windows confirmed peak/average/late displaying correctly
- Duration counter now increments by 1s per tick (previously 2s when
  the old alternating approach was used)

## Relation to latency_reporting branch

The existing origin/latency_reporting branch contains an earlier attempt at
latency reporting on the pre-refactor codebase (info_mcast_server.rs was
at inferno_aoip/src/ rather than inferno_aoip/src/device_server/). That
approach also used combined packets with 0x8004 commented out, which is why
it did not produce visible latency stats in DC. This commit implements the
correct two-datagram pattern on the current dev branch structure.
